WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.930
MIKE COLEMAN: Hi.

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:04.200
I'm Mike Coleman, a developer
advocate here at Google Cloud.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.970
In this video, we're
going to take a look

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.430
at what might be the quickest
way to move workloads running

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:09.990
in VMs onto containers.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.580
Specifically, I'll
be showing you

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.290
how you can utilize
our migration

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:16.470
tools to move your existing
VM-based workloads to Cloud

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:20.242
Run, Google Cloud's fully
managed serverless platform.

00:00:20.242 --> 00:00:23.546
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.530
Before we get into the details
on Cloud Run and our migration

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:31.960
tooling, let's level
set by what we mean when

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:33.550
we use the term "serverless."

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:35.620
Serverless platforms
allow developers

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:38.560
to abstract infrastructure
management for a simpler

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.150
developer experience.

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:43.450
With serverless tools, tasks
like managing the underlying

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:45.610
infrastructure are
handled by Google Cloud.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:48.250
This infrastructure scales
dynamically as needed.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:50.290
By handling these
day-to-day tasks,

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:52.180
you're freed up to focus
on additional value

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:54.190
added tasks, which
can ultimately

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:56.320
increase your speed to market.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.270
Cloud Run allows you
to develop and deploy

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:00.430
highly scalable,
containerised applications

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:02.736
on a fully managed
serverless platform that

00:01:02.736 --> 00:01:04.569
allows you to deploy
containerised workloads

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:06.440
in seconds.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.200
In addition to
reduced complexity,

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.790
Cloud Run also offers
several key benefits,

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:15.520
including increased security via
automatic HTTPS and flexibility

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.560
with custom domains.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.200
You can write your code using
your favorite language--

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.590
Go, Python, Java,
Ruby, and more.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:27.310
It's built upon the container
and Knative open standards,

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:30.970
enabling portability
of your applications.

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:33.550
And you can abstract away
all infrastructure management

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.250
for a simpler
developer experience.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.650
When looking at modernizing
existing workloads,

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:41.150
I like to break it down
into two big buckets.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.570
The first is the modernization
of the underlying

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:47.050
infrastructure, such as
running workloads on Cloud Run.

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:50.020
The second is modernizing
your existing workloads.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.450
One way to do that is to
take an application that

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:54.820
is running inside of
a VM and convert it

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.040
to run inside of a container.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.930
Migrate for GKE
and Serverless is

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:01.150
a set of services that
helps with that process

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:04.700
by automating a significant
chunk of that work.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.860
When looking at migrating
an existing workload,

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.979
there are four discrete steps
that you'll want to go through.

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:12.200
First, discovery
and assessment--

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.180
Migrate has a fit
assessment tool

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:15.650
that can be used
to help identify

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:17.570
what workloads you have
running and which are

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.280
best candidates for migration.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:20.810
Take a look at
our fit assessment

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.160
video for more information.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.220
Next is the setup
and planning phase.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.650
Migrated workloads can
be run on Cloud Run,

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:31.410
as well as in GKE in Standard
or Autopilot mode, or on Anthos.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:35.240
Additionally, you can
orchestrate migration pipelines

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.740
to help automate the
migration process.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:40.740
In this video, we're
focused on the third phase.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.780
So I won't spend a lot
of time on it right here.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.510
The final phase deals with
how you manage applications

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:47.830
after they've been migrated.

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:50.140
Because Migrate for
GKE and Serverless

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.300
provides you with the
resulting Docker file,

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.760
you can easily manage
updates to your code

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.490
via standard
deployment pipelines.

00:02:57.490 --> 00:02:59.350
To illustrate how
Migrate for Serverless

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.510
works with Cloud Run,
I'm going to show you

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:04.090
a demo of taking a Django
application's web front

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.940
end from a VM and
running it on Cloud Run.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:08.950
We're actually joining
the migration process

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:09.730
in the middle.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:12.520
The application was
originally a monolithic VM

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:15.310
that had a front end and the
database running together

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:16.480
in the single VM.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.180
We use Google Cloud's
Database Migration Service

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:21.010
to move the database
to Cloud SQL,

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:23.890
and the web front
end is still in a VM.

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:26.440
In this demo, I'll use
Migrate for Serverless

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:28.780
to move the Django
application out of the VM

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:32.110
and into a container that
I will deploy to Cloud Run.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:35.470
To start the demo, I'll provide
Migrate for GKE and Serverless

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:36.640
a source VM.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.770
The Migrate processes
will inspect the VM

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:40.420
and then generate
a migration plan

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.040
that I will edit as needed.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:44.360
From there, it will generate
the deployment artifacts,

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.180
including a Docker container.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:49.235
Once we have that container,
I'll deploy that to Cloud Run.

00:03:49.235 --> 00:03:50.860
With that background,
let's take a look

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:53.360
at the process in action.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:55.880
Let's start by taking a look
at our application in action.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:59.120
So it's basically just a
database of Unicode characters.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:00.520
So if I click on
the waving hand,

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.130
I can see different characters
for different operating

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:03.850
systems.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:05.680
Now, because this is
Django application,

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.610
we are using Nginx and
Gunicorn in tandem,

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:11.740
where we have Gunicorn
running on a socket,

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:14.230
and we bind that to a port
that Nginx is listening on.

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:16.870
We're going to need to change
that in our application.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:18.399
And all of that is
being served out

00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:19.570
of this virtual
machine, which I'm

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:20.860
going to shut down right now.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:23.870
Shutting down the VM is a
prerequisite to migrating it.

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:26.110
So let me stop that
VM, and we'll get on

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:29.830
with the actual migration.

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:32.810
With the VM stopped, let's go
ahead and create our migration.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:35.740
So I'll click Create Migration,
give the migration a name,

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:38.200
select a source, which
I created earlier,

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:40.180
choose Linux system
container for the workload

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:43.270
type, the Django GCE
instance, and we're just going

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:44.870
to migrate the base image.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:46.703
So that's going to take
a couple of minutes.

00:04:46.703 --> 00:04:48.412
I'm going to go ahead
and stop the video,

00:04:48.412 --> 00:04:49.960
and I'll join you
when it's finished.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.002
All right, we're finished
now, so let me go ahead

00:04:52.002 --> 00:04:53.710
and review and edit
my migration plan.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:55.085
The first thing
I need to do is I

00:04:55.085 --> 00:04:57.220
need to set this value
to True so that we

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:01.000
can use our enhanced runtime
and run on Cloud Run.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.280
I also want to disable
all these services.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.252
Some of these are used for
that Gunicorn Nginx pattern

00:05:08.252 --> 00:05:09.460
that we talked about earlier.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:11.140
Other are services
that I don't actually

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.480
want to run in
production, such as SSH.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:15.745
So I'm going to
disable all of these.

00:05:15.745 --> 00:05:17.620
And when I have all
of those disabled,

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:20.410
I can come down here and click
Save and generate artifacts.

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:23.050
That's going to generate
the Docker file as well

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:26.140
as a couple of Docker container
images, which I will eventually

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:27.820
deploy onto Cloud Run.

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:29.230
So let me go ahead down here.

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:30.970
I'll click Save and
generate artifacts.

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:33.400
Again, this takes
a little bit, so I

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:37.600
will fast-forward the video and
rejoin you when it's finished.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:39.342
OK, we've generated
those artifacts.

00:05:39.342 --> 00:05:41.050
So let's go ahead and
move back over here

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:42.717
and take a look at
what we've generated.

00:05:42.717 --> 00:05:45.742
So if I click on Review
Artifacts, we can come in.

00:05:45.742 --> 00:05:47.200
And we talked about
before-- here's

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.960
a Docker file, a
Kubernetes deployment spec,

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:51.850
as well as the
container image base

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:53.350
layer and the runtime layer.

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:56.590
So we're going to go ahead and
move into Cloud Shell here.

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:58.305
I'll use migctl to
download my artifacts.

00:05:58.305 --> 00:06:00.430
And I can list them out
here, and you can see them.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:04.810
Now, services-config.yaml is a
file that we can use to define

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:07.090
services that we want to
run in our new container.

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:10.750
And I'm going to use that to
define the Gunicorn service

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:11.260
ingress.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.510
So this replaces sort of
the systemd configuration

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:15.640
we looked at earlier.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:18.760
Since I want to include
this updated services-config

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:21.730
in my new container, I'm going
to go ahead and run Cloud Build

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:23.373
to rebuild my container image.

00:06:23.373 --> 00:06:25.540
And that's going to incorporate
that services-config

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:27.610
and give me a new version
of my container image

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:29.050
that I can deploy
into Cloud Run.

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:30.340
So let's let this finish here.

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:33.120
I'll speed it up a little bit.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:34.710
With that container
image created,

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:37.440
let's move into Cloud Run and
create a new Cloud Run service.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:38.732
So we're going to come in here.

00:06:38.732 --> 00:06:40.690
We choose that container
image we just updated.

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:42.440
We're going to scroll
down and we're going

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:44.130
to set an environment variable.

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:46.350
That environment
variable tells Cloud Run

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:49.200
that we're using the enhanced
runtime from the Migrate

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.480
for Containers system.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:52.390
So let's go in and
set that to True.

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:53.765
Then we're going
to come in here.

00:06:53.765 --> 00:06:56.520
We're going to define the
connection to our Cloud SQL

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:58.030
database.

00:06:58.030 --> 00:06:59.350
So we'll select that there.

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:00.150
And then we're going
to come in here

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:02.820
and we're going to allow
unauthenticated invocations so

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:05.303
that we can reach the
service more easily.

00:07:05.303 --> 00:07:06.720
This will take a
second to deploy,

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.760
so let's let it run
through its thing.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:09.840
We've got the URL.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:12.600
I'm going to paste it up
here and create a new tab.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.000
Let me grab that, and new tab.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.880
And there's our application
running on Cloud Run.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:21.143
You can see everything working
as you would expect it to.

00:07:21.143 --> 00:07:22.560
Well, I hope this
video showed you

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:24.810
how you can use Google
Cloud's Migrate tooling

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:28.080
to move applications out
of VMs and into Cloud Run.

